// How a program tells how long something took, and how it waits.
//
// clock_ns() is now on a clock that only goes forward: nanoseconds since
// some point — the system's start, not a date. How long something took is
// the difference of two: `start := clock_ns()`, the work, then
// `clock_ns() - start`. (A date and a time of day are another clock, which
// can be set back: not this one.)
//
// sleep_ns(ns) waits: the program does nothing for that long — at least,
// and a little more, as the system wakes it: a fraction of a millisecond.
// sleep_ms(ms) is the same in milliseconds. (On Windows a high-resolution
// timer: a plain Sleep there rounds a wait up to the system's tick, 15.6 ms,
// a whole frame at 60 a second.)
//
// mara.time builds a game loop's timing on them: a Timer holds when a frame
// started and how long the last one took (tally_up), a Fixed_Timer turns
// that into fixed steps of game time (one_step), and sleep(work, fps) waits
// out the rest of a frame.
//
// This program times some work, then paces three frames at 60 a second.
module timing

use mara.core
use mara.os
use mara.time

main :: fun() {
	// How long a loop takes.
	start := clock_ns()
	sum : i64 = 0
	for i in 0 ..< 1000000 {
		sum += i % 7
	}
	took := clock_ns() - start
	print("a million steps:", took / 1000, "microseconds; their sum:", sum)

	// Three frames: the work, then the rest of a 60th of a second asleep.
	frame := 1000000000 / 60
	for f in 0 ..< 3 {
		frame_start := clock_ns()
		// (a frame's work goes here)
		rest := frame - (clock_ns() - frame_start)
		sleep_ns(rest)
		print("frame", f, "took", (clock_ns() - frame_start) / 1000, "microseconds")
	}

	// The same with mara.time's Timer: its counter set when the work starts.
	work := Timer()
	work.counter = time_now()
	sleep(work, 60.0)
	seconds, _ := time_since(work.counter)
	print("mara.time's frame:", seconds, "seconds")
}
